<section class="section narrow prose" id="rules">
  <h2>How it works, and the rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You fund a test; you get the result either way.</strong> We promise to run the test and publish what it finds, including when it refutes the idea. We never promise the outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>The question and its falsifier are fixed first.</strong> Each page shows the result that would prove the idea wrong, written before the run.</li>
    <li><strong>Earmarking is by link.</strong> GitHub Sponsors has no per-payment memo, so use the "fund this direction" link on the page. It carries the direction's id.</li>
    <li><strong>Priority, not a purchase.</strong> Money moves a direction up our research agent's ranking. A fully funded direction runs next. A partly funded one waits for the budget to fill.</li>
    <li><strong>If a direction never fills,</strong> what was given for it rolls over to the general research fund. We do not refund by default, and we say so before you give.</li>
    <li><strong>Sponsorship, not donation.</strong> Unless a fiscal host makes it a donation, these payments are sponsorship of open research, not tax-deductible gifts. Ask your own adviser.</li>
    <li><strong>Costs are estimates</strong> from the trial's planned hours times a rate. If a run costs less, the remainder goes to the general fund.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
